#NOTE: This is a lessons learned review from TryHackMe's SOC L1 - Systems as Attack Vectors lab

#While preparing users for attacks is always a good thing, it isn't the end all be all. A castle gate with a cheap padlock isn't secure as one with a vaulted lock and barricades.

#Protecting systems is the key to cyber defense. Failing to do so can result in attackers stealing data, exploiting companies via blackmail, or using compromised systems to form a 
#botnet.

#81% of breaches were the direct result of lost or stolen passwords. Users should never reuse passwords, share passwords, or fail to renew passwords off of standardized renewal times.

#Every piece of software has vulnerabilities. The problem is whether or not we know about them.

#Attackers will exploit software vulnerabilities wherever possible. They can even utilize flaws in the system that aren't known yet to defenders. These are called "Zero-Days."

#Whenever a major exploit is discovered, it is best to set up an IPS or WAF to prevent data loss until a patch to the vulnerability can be applied.

#Misconfigurations are oftentimes dangerous flaws that could be prevented. Resetting a manufacturer's password, changing the default settings, or performing penetration tests can
#help to explain where the attackers could exploit the defended system.

